.TH CHTTPSVR_ENGINE_WAIT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_engine_wait \- wait for the engine thread to exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_engine_wait(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until the shared reactor of chttpserver has
stopped completely, whether the stop was started before this call, at
the same time as this call, or some time after it.  If nothing ever
stops the reactor, this function blocks forever; for example, an
external SIGTERM handler may call chttpsvr_engine_stop() at a later,
unknown time.

Dropping the last reference to this reactor, which happens when you
destroy the last chttpsvr with chttpsvr_destroy, starts an
asynchronous teardown that does not block the caller that started it.
This function is the explicit escape hatch for a program that must
synchronize with that shutdown, or with a chttpsvr_engine_stop() call
that comes from an external signal.  This reactor is separate from the
engine of chttpclient, so this function has no connection to the
async work of chttpclient in the same process.

It can be called concurrently from more than one thread, and each
caller blocks until the library confirms that the reactor has stopped
completely, regardless of which caller started the stop, whether by a
release or by a chttpsvr_engine_stop() call.

Do not call this function from a request handler or a middleware that
runs on the worker pool of ANY chttpsvr instance.  Such a call is a
FATAL error: it calls ccol_fatal_err(), which means abort() and
SIGABRT, so the library reports it at once instead of hanging.  The
drain of the worker pool of that server can never finish while this
in-flight request itself blocks here waiting for the engine to exit,
which would deadlock the shutdown of the whole shared engine, not just
of one server.  An admin or shutdown endpoint that wants a full drain
before it answers calls chttpsvr_engine_stop() and then returns; call
this function from a separate thread.

After this function returns, the fallback engine logger is closed.  A
logger installed with chttpsvr_set_engine_logger stays in place, and
the engine that a later chttpsvr_start brings up logs through it
again.

It is safe to call this function before the reactor has ever started;
it then does nothing.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_set_engine_logger (3)
